steps.body:
Union find keeps a `parent` for every item in a `std::vector<int>`. Items that belong together form a tree, and the item at the top, the one that is its own parent, is the group's **root**. At the start every item is alone, its own parent:

```cpp
std::vector<int> parent(5);
std::iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
for (int p : parent) {
    std::cout << p << ' ';   // 0 1 2 3 4: everyone is a root
}
```

To find an item's group, follow parents up to the root. Two items are in the same group exactly when they reach the same root. To join two groups, find both roots and hang one under the other:

```cpp harness
{{code}}
std::vector<int> parent = {0, 1, 2, 3, 4};
unite(parent, 0, 1);
unite(parent, 3, 4);
unite(parent, 1, 4);
std::cout << std::boolalpha << (findRoot(parent, 4) == findRoot(parent, 0)) << ' ' << (findRoot(parent, 2) == findRoot(parent, 0)) << '\n';
```

```cpp
int findRoot(const std::vector<int>& parent, int x) {
    while (parent[x] != x) {
        x = parent[x];       // one step up
    }
    return x;
}

void unite(std::vector<int>& parent, int a, int b) {
    int ra = findRoot(parent, a);
    int rb = findRoot(parent, b);
    if (ra != rb) {
        parent[rb] = ra;     // b's whole group joins a's
    }
}
```

Hang a root, never `a` or `b` themselves: `parent[b] = a` would cut `b` away from the rest of its group. Which item ends up on top doesn't matter, only that everyone in a group reaches the same one. The name is `unite` because `union` is a C++ keyword, the one *Binary Formats: Bit-Fields, Unions, and Endianness* used.

example.code:
