steps.body:
Walking up is slow when a group is a long chain: `findRoot` on the bottom item takes a step for every item above it. **Path compression** fixes that while it walks. Once `findRoot` knows the root, it points every item it passed straight at the root, so the next search from any of them takes one step:

```cpp harness
{{code}}
std::vector<int> parent = {1, 2, 3, 3};   // the chain 0 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3
std::cout << findRoot(parent, 0) << ':';
for (int p : parent) {
    std::cout << ' ' << p;
}
std::cout << '\n';
```

```cpp
int findRoot(std::vector<int>& parent, int x) {
    if (parent[x] != x) {
        parent[x] = findRoot(parent, parent[x]);   // point x at the root
    }
    return parent[x];
}
```

The recursion climbs to the root, then on the way back sets each item's parent to the root it got back. `parent` is passed by plain reference now, since finding changes it. For the chain `0 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3`, one `findRoot(parent, 0)` turns `parent` from `1 2 3 3` into `3 3 3 3`.

The groups don't change, only the shape of each tree. Together with union by size from the next part, a find costs nearly O(1) on average, however many items there are. The first find down a very long chain still recurses once per item, but every find leaves the trees flatter.

