steps.body:
Path compression flattens trees after the fact. **Union by size** keeps them flat from the start: keep a `size` for every root, and when two groups join, hang the smaller root under the bigger one. An item only moves a level down when its group at least doubles, so no tree gets deeper than about log n. Put both vectors and both operations in a struct:

```cpp harness
{{code}}
UnionFind uf(5);
uf.unite(0, 1);
uf.unite(2, 1);
std::cout << uf.size[uf.findRoot(2)] << ' ' << std::boolalpha << uf.unite(0, 2) << '\n';
```

```cpp
struct UnionFind {
    std::vector<int> parent;
    std::vector<int> size;

    explicit UnionFind(int n) : parent(n), size(n, 1) {
        std::iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
    }

    int findRoot(int x) {
        if (parent[x] != x) {
            parent[x] = findRoot(parent[x]);
        }
        return parent[x];
    }

    bool unite(int a, int b) {
        int ra = findRoot(a);
        int rb = findRoot(b);
        if (ra == rb) {
            return false;            // already in one group
        }
        if (size[ra] < size[rb]) {
            std::swap(ra, rb);       // now ra is the bigger root
        }
        parent[rb] = ra;
        size[ra] += size[rb];
        return true;
    }
};
```

Only a root's `size` stays up to date, so read a group's size as `size[findRoot(x)]`. `unite` returns `false` when the two items were already together, which the next part puts to use.

